Message-ID: <d19960c3fdd80eefb4911894714da6de79f2ea87.1786678766.git.dongchenchen2@huawei.com>
In-Reply-To: <cover.1786678766.git.dongchenchen2@huawei.com>
References: <cover.1786678766.git.dongchenchen2@huawei.com>
From: Dong Chenchen <dongchenchen2@huawei.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 14:20:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH openEuler-26.09 19/22] mm: control i_mmap shards from the command line

hulk inclusion
category: feature
bugzilla: https://atomgit.com/openeuler/kernel/issues/9771

--------------------------------

Selecting CONFIG_I_MMAP_SHARDS currently makes sharding active
automatically. That requires separate kernel images for central-tree and
sharded operation, while a boolean check in the shard installation path
still leaves counting, pointer loads, and wrapper calls in the disabled
path.

Turn CONFIG_I_MMAP_SHARDS into a hidden platform capability on MMU,
SMP, NUMA kernels and leave the feature disabled by default. Enable it
only when i_mmap_shards=on is parsed during early boot. Keep the switch
boot-only so the tree layout cannot change policy while mappings are
live.

Use a default-false static key in the writer wrappers, shard pointer
loads, VMA accounting, mapping_mapped(), and inode and mm initialization.
The disabled path then takes the central lock directly and avoids shard
state accesses and atomic accounting. Remove the extra shard pointer from
the write-lock context and derive it from the selected root on the enabled
slow path.

Finally, check the static key again at the shard publication point so a
future caller cannot install shards while the feature is disabled.

Signed-off-by: Dong Chenchen <dongchenchen2@huawei.com>
